            <ArticleTitle>Prevention of re-fractures of both bones of the forearm in children.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>872-875</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of this study was to observe if the re-fracture rate after forearm both bones was decreased by protecting the forearm with a preventive brace for 6 months following the fracture and by ceasing all physical activities.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We performed a retrospective study in 75 consecutive cases of diaphyseal fracture of both bone of forearms, in 52 boys and 23 girls aged from 6 months to 11 years. It concerned a first episode of fracture in 84% of cases and a re-fracture in 17%.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Re-fracture rate was 0% in the group where a protective brace was worn while it was 20% in the group without brace.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Both the wearing of a protective brace and absence of sport for 6 months may decrease the re-fracture rate to 0%, if the patient is compliant by wearing it and by ceasing all physical activities.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
